messages:
sophia: Heads up — our sample-apps CI is red for the Cloudflare Pages / edge target. Error: "SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module" when the app imports our published package's ESM entry.

deploy-bot: build failed: sample-app-edge (job #842) — exit code 1 — logs uploaded: https://ci.internal/redwood/842/logs

leo: Can you paste the package.json from the published tarball and the import line the sample app uses?

sophia: Sure — here's the relevant bits from package.json we published (simplified):
```json
{
  "main": "dist/index.cjs",
  "module": "dist/index.mjs",
  "types": "dist/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "node": {
        "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
        "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
      },
      "default": "./dist/index.cjs"
    },
    "./edge": {
      "import": "./dist/edge.mjs",
      "default": "./dist/edge.cjs"
    }
  }
}
```
And sample-app does: `import { createClient } from 'redwood-inference'` (no subpath).

ben: This is a classic resolution mismatch. Edge runtimes + some bundlers (esbuild via Cloudflare Pages) will treat packages differently — they might not honor the `node` condition and fall back to `default` or to UMD-style resolution. Also: TS tooling doesn't reliably read conditional `exports` for types. Top-level `types` helps, but consumers still get the wrong runtime entry in edge.

tess: Short term: we can expose an explicit edge-friendly entrypoint like `redwood-inference/edge` and tell sample-apps to import that. Long term: we should tweak `exports` to include a `browser`/`worker` condition so edge environments pick the ESM worker build. Eg: `"exports": { ".": { "browser":"./dist/edge.mjs", "import":"./dist/index.mjs", "require":"./dist/index.cjs" } }`

ciara: Related bug: the edge build we shipped doesn’t include the retry wrapper used in node — I traced a customer issue where transient 401s (token refresh race) weren't retried on edge. Node path wraps requests with `fetchWithRetry`, edge path calls `fetch` directly. That leads to more flaky failures on short-lived tokens.

ben: Ouch. That explains differences in reported auth flakiness between hosted vs edge. We should have parity on auth+retry semantics regardless of runtime.

leo: Are we comfortable shipping a tiny runtime-agnostic retry util (no deps) that uses the global `fetch` when present and falls back to `node-fetch` for Node? We can keep it internal and tree-shakeable.

ciara: Yep — I prototyped a small util:
```js
async function fetchWithRetry(input, opts, retries = 2) {
  for (let i = 0; i <= retries; i++) {
    try {
      const res = await fetch(input, opts);
      if (!res.ok && i < retries) continue;
      return res;
    } catch (e) {
      if (i === retries) throw e;
    }
  }
}
module.exports = { fetchWithRetry }
```
It works in worker and node (with a `global.fetch` polyfill).

sophia: Good. Typings question: TypeScript consumers in sample apps lost intellisense because some bundlers resolved to the CJS file and TS couldn't map types. Reminder: TS primarily looks at top-level `types` or `exports` subpath mappings; it doesn't always infer types from conditional branches. So we must keep a top-level `types` that points to `dist/index.d.ts`. Also consider adding explicit subpath types if we expose `/edge`.

tess: Action plan proposal: 
- expose explicit entrypoint `/edge` for immediate fix and update sample-app imports (ben)
- add `browser`/`worker` condition into `exports` and publish a patch (tess)
- standardize auth/retry by migrating both node+edge to the tiny `fetchWithRetry` util (ciara)
- ensure top-level `types` and add `types` files for subpaths (sophia)

ben: I'll update sample-apps to import `/edge` so CI is green and open a PR to change their bootstrap.

ciara: I'll send a PR with the retry util + a couple unit tests + a README note about polyfills needed in older node.

sophia: I'll open the package.json changes PR (exports/browser + top-level types) and coordinate a canary publish. Once canary looks good we'll merge and cut a patch.

leo: Sounds good — please @-me on the PRs. Also we should add a short note to onboarding docs: "If you're targeting edge runtimes import from 'redwood-inference/edge' until final exports are stable."

ben: @sophia @ciara @tess noted — will take sample-apps now. :thumbsup:

deploy-bot: CI note — reopening job #842 once sample-apps PR is merged will auto-run edge build tests.

sophia: Thanks everyone — I'll file follow-ups and link PRs in this thread.

leo: :raised_hands:
